# It works. So why is anyone still saying no?
next: {"title":"The patients you never meet: opportunity cost as the hidden loser","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/the-patients-you-never-meet-opportunity.cast"}

## It works. So why is anyone still saying no?
voice: male
level: basic
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: The two halves of the question
    x_label: Incremental effect, ΔE (QALYs)
    y_label: Incremental cost, ΔC (kr)
    wtp_threshold: 275000
    points:
      - {label: Nusinersen (SMA), effect: 2, cost: 6000000, emphasize: true}
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 1, cost: 80000}

Over four parts, this series answers one question: when a treatment plainly works, how can a health system still say no? Everything turns on two axes — along here, the health a treatment adds, measured in QALYs.
    draw x_axis

And upward, what it costs. Delta E and delta C: the effect and the cost of a new technology, both measured against current care.
    draw y_axis

    pause 1

    ask "What should I call you?" store name default friend
        intro: One question before we begin.

Four quadrants, {name}: cheaper or costlier, better or worse than what we already do.
    draw q_ne q_nw q_se q_sw parallel true

Almost every new drug worth arguing about lands in one of them — the top right, where it works and it costs more.
    erase q_nw q_se q_sw

    pause 1

    erase q_ne

Our running example: nusinersen, sold as Spinraza, for spinal muscular atrophy — in its severe infant form, a disease that used to kill or permanently ventilate most children before their second birthday.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

And it works: in the pivotal infant trial, none of the untreated babies reached the motor milestones, while about half of the treated ones did.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

    pause 1

Now the half of the question no efficacy trial asks. This line is lambda — Norway's rough willingness to pay, around 275,000 kroner per QALY for a gain of ordinary alvorlighet, that is, severity.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

Nusinersen sat far above it: at list price, millions of kroner per patient per year, and delta C over delta E — the ICER — landed in the millions per QALY, roughly ten times the line.
    point at.ref pt_0 gesture circle

    pause 1

For contrast, here, almost hugging the origin, sits a hip replacement — about one QALY gained for something like eighty thousand kroner.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

Zoom in, and it rests quietly below the line: unglamorous, high-volume, no headlines, and far more health for every krone.
    camera center.ref pt_1 zoom 3

Pull back out. The we that pays for the dot up there is one budget — four regional health authorities deciding jointly in Beslutningsforum — so a krone spent here cannot be spent there.
    camera reset true

So the honest question is nytte and ressursbruk together — benefit and resource use — and that binds any payer with a fixed budget, a private clinic as much as a public system.
    mark mark_lambda target wtp_label kind box

Beslutningsforum first said no to nusinersen, then yes for patients under eighteen once the price came down — the dot moved, the evidence didn't.
    draw title

    pause 1

It works, and someone still says no, because a yes is never a claim about the effect alone — it is a claim about where the effect sits relative to its price. Next: why effect alone can never settle it.
    delivery grave

## Effect is not enough
voice: male
level: basic

    text caption "Nusinersen (1) vs current care (0) — one patient, lifetime, discounted" #8f887c x 500 y 700 font_size 22 hidden true
    text col0_head "Current care (0)" #2f6b8f x 530 y 645 font_size 28 hidden true
    text col1_head "Nusinersen (1)" #b5482e x 800 y 645 font_size 28 hidden true
    path head_rule #8f887c points [[80,610],[930,610]] style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true
    path col_divider dashed #8f887c points [[665,600],[665,415]] style.stroke_width 1.2 hidden true
    text row_e_label "Effect  E  (QALYs)" x 210 y 545 font_size 26 hidden true
    text e0 "12.0" #2f6b8f x 530 y 545 font_size 32 hidden true
    text e1 "16.0" #b5482e x 800 y 545 font_size 32 hidden true
    text row_c_label "Cost  C  (kr)" x 210 y 455 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c0 "2 mill" #2f6b8f x 530 y 455 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c1 "22 mill" #b5482e x 800 y 455 font_size 32 hidden true
    path mid_rule #8f887c points [[80,410],[930,410]] style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true

Now that we have seen what the question is actually asking, let's run one real drug through it — nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy, set against current care.
    draw caption col0_head col1_head head_rule col_divider

    pause 0.9

Effect first, in quality-adjusted life years: about twelve on current care, about sixteen with the drug — round numbers, but close to the spirit of the Norwegian appraisal.
    draw row_e_label e0 e1

    pause 1

So ΔE is plus four QALYs: a real, visible gain, and nobody in this debate claims the drug does nothing.
    text dE "ΔE = +4 QALYs" x 300 y 355 font_size 30

    pause 0.8

    ask "Nusinersen clearly works. In one word — should the health system pay for it?" store verdict default yes
        intro: A quick check before we go further.

You answered '{verdict}'. Hold on to that — because nothing on the canvas so far can justify it, in either direction.
    point at.ref dE gesture circle

    pause 0.9

Here is the other half of the ledger: cost over the same horizon — a couple of million kroner on current care, around twenty-two million with the drug.
    draw mid_rule row_c_label c0 c1

    pause 0.9

ΔC is plus twenty million kroner, for a single patient — a treatment priced in millions per year, continuing for life.
    text dC "ΔC = +20 million kr" x 730 y 355 font_size 30

    pause 1

Put the two together and you have the ICER: ΔC divided by ΔE, roughly five million kroner for each extra good year of life.
    text icer "ICER = ΔC / ΔE ≈ 5 million kr per QALY" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 250 font_size 32

This number, and not the effect, is what a decision-maker actually sits and stares at.
    camera center.ref icer zoom 1.9 delivery grave

    camera reset true

    pause 0.8

    clear

So hold that twenty million kroner fixed, and ask the question that comes next: what else would it have bought?
    text same_money "The same 20 million kroner" x 500 y 650 font_size 34

    pause 0.9

Spent on nusinersen, it buys those four quality-adjusted life years, for one child.
    path bar_a #b5482e points [[120,520],[132,520]] style.stroke_width 24
    text lab_a "4 QALYs — one child on nusinersen" #b5482e x 330 y 520 font_size 26

    pause 0.9

Spent on hip replacements — roughly a hundred thousand kroner each, about one good year of life each — the very same money buys around two hundred.
    path bar_b #8a5fa8 points [[120,420],[720,420]] style.stroke_width 24
    text lab_b "≈ 200 QALYs — about 200 hip replacements" #8a5fa8 x 420 y 368 font_size 26

Fifty times the health, from an operation so ordinary that it never makes the news.
    highlight bar_b effect glow

    pause 1

That is why a positive ΔE settles nothing: the question was never whether it works, but whether it works better per krone than what the krone is already doing.
    text verdict_text "A positive ΔE decides nothing on its own" x 500 y 270 font_size 30
    mark verdict_box target verdict_text kind box

    pause 0.8

And this is no public-sector quirk — a private clinic with a fixed budget and a full operating list runs exactly the same arithmetic, because every yes is a no somewhere else.
    text clinic "A private clinic with a fixed budget faces the same arithmetic" #8f887c x 500 y 170 font_size 24

    pause 0.9

So when someone still says no to a drug that plainly works, they are not denying the effect — they are pricing it, at five million kroner a year, against everything that money could otherwise have healed.
    delivery grave

## Who pays, and at what price per QALY
voice: male
level: basic
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: What the shared purse is asked to buy
    x_label: Incremental effect, ΔE (QALYs)
    y_label: Incremental cost, ΔC (kr)
    wtp_threshold: 825000
    points:
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 2, cost: 60000}
      - {label: 'Nusinersen, list price', effect: 2.5, cost: 20000000, emphasize: true}
      - {label: 'Under 18, negotiated price', effect: 6, cost: 9000000}

Now that we have seen that a real effect is not enough, the blunt question is who actually pays — and what that payer can carry.
    draw x_axis y_axis

    draw q_nw q_ne q_sw q_se parallel true

The 'we' here is a shared purse: tax money, spent through blåresept — the blue-prescription scheme — and, for hospital drugs like this one, by the four regional health trusts sitting in Beslutningsforum.
    draw title

    pause 1

    ask "In kroner, what should a health system be willing to pay for one extra year in full health?" store limit default "800 000"
        intro: Before we put a line on this plane, one question for you.

You said {limit} kroner. Norway's own ceiling — lambda — reaches about 825,000 kroner per QALY, and only for the most severe conditions; that is the slope of this line.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

Below the line the purse buys a QALY for less than it has said one is worth; above it, it does not — and without such a line, 'worth it' is settled by whoever argues loudest.
    highlight wtp_line effect glow

    pause 1

Nusinersen: a list price of roughly 750,000 kroner per injection, six injections in the first year and three every year after — several million kroner per patient, every year.
    draw pt_1

Over the modelled horizon that is something like twenty million kroner more than current care, for around two and a half QALYs gained.
    draw pt_label_1

Delta C over delta E: on these simplified numbers, about eight million kroner per QALY — roughly ten times the highest ceiling Norway has ever written down.
    point at.ref pt_1 gesture circle delivery grave

    pause 1

And here, apparently sitting on the origin, is a hip replacement — two good QALYs for a few tens of thousands of kroner.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

Zoom in, and it separates from zero: comfortably under the line, bought quietly, thousands of times a year, with no headlines.
    camera center.ref pt_0 zoom 5

Zoom back out, and the drug's price sets a scale on which an entire operation becomes invisible.
    camera reset true

    pause 1

In 2017, Beslutningsforum said no to nusinersen — a rejection of a drug that visibly worked, and the country argued about it for a year.
    erase q_nw q_ne q_sw q_se

In February 2018 the same forum said yes — but only for patients under eighteen, and only after a negotiated price that remains confidential.
    draw pt_2 pt_label_2 delivery soft

Two things moved the point: a smaller delta C from the price cut, and a larger delta E, because children treated early gain far more good years than adults do.
    point at.ref pt_2 gesture tap

    pause 1

So the yes was not a change of mind about the evidence — it was a change in the price, and in who gets the drug.
    mark mark_yes target pt_label_2 kind box

The 'we' is a shared budget with a written ceiling, so 'worth it' never means 'does it work' — it means a price per QALY this purse can honestly carry, for these patients, at this price.

## What a 'no' actually means
voice: male
level: basic
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: What a 'no' actually means
    wtp_threshold: 275000
    x_label: ΔE — incremental effect (QALYs)
    y_label: ΔC — incremental cost (kr)
    points:
      - {label: Nusinersen (SMA), effect: 3, cost: 3000000, emphasize: true}
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 1.5, cost: 100000}

Now that we have seen who the 'we' is — everyone sharing one fixed budget — we can put the whole argument on a single figure, and answer the question this lecture opened with: it works, so why is anyone still saying no?
    draw x_axis

Horizontally, ΔE: the extra quality-adjusted life years a new technology buys over current care. Vertically, ΔC: the extra kroner it costs. The origin is simply what we do today.
    draw y_axis

    pause 1

    ask "What should I call you?" store name default friend
        intro: One small thing before we read it.

{name}, almost every interesting decision lands in the top-right quadrant: more effective, and more expensive. That is where the whole discipline earns its keep.
    draw q_nw q_ne q_sw q_se parallel true

    pause 1

    erase q_ne

Here is nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy: in round illustrative numbers, about three million kroner more per patient, for roughly three extra quality-adjusted life years.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

And the effect is real and visible — infants who would never have sat unaided, sitting. Nobody in the Norwegian debate ever denied that.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

    pause 1

Now the unglamorous comparison we keep returning to: a hip replacement, around a hundred thousand kroner for perhaps one and a half good years — it barely lifts off the axis.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

    pause 1

This dashed line through the origin is the threshold, lambda: in Norway around 275,000 kroner per good life year — the rate at which the same money is thought to buy health somewhere else in the system.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

    pause 1

Zoom in on the gap. At three QALYs the line stands at roughly eight hundred thousand kroner; the drug asks for three million — and severity, alvorlighet, lifts that line for a child, but nowhere near that far.
    camera center.ref pt_0 zoom 1.5

    pause 1

Which is exactly what happened: Beslutningsforum said no in 2017, and yes the following year — restricted by age, and only after the price came down.
    camera reset true

Strip the quadrants away and one rule is left underneath all of it.
    erase q_nw q_sw q_se

Net monetary benefit: lambda times ΔE, minus ΔC. Positive, and the purchase buys more health than it displaces; negative, and it does not — and without that arithmetic, the loudest, most photogenic case simply takes the money.
    text rule "NMB = λ × ΔE − ΔC" x 500 y 48 font_size 30
    mark rule_box target rule kind box

    pause 1

So a 'no' is not the claim that the drug does not work, {name}. It is the claim that these same kroner, spent on the dull point down here, would have bought more health for someone else.
    focus pt_1 wtp_line

    pause 1

That is the series in one line: 'worth it' is never effect alone, the 'we' who pays is a fixed budget, and so every yes is a purchase made with other patients' health.
    draw title delivery grave

Which leaves the obvious question for next time — who exactly are those patients we never meet?

## Where next
end_page: true

Next: The patients you never meet: opportunity cost as the hidden loser
    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 545 font_size 24 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_next href lecture:2 title "The patients you never meet: opportunity cost as the hidden loser" size 320 x 500 y 395

    link end_again href lecture:1 title "Watch again" form text open here x 500 y 160
    text end_count "1 of 20" x 500 y 95 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
